warsi for BlankOn
=================

Where the source comes from
---------------------------

Warsi is BlankOn Linux's app store: Bazaar, heavily patched and rebranded
(app ID id.blankonlinux.warsi). The upstream source is BlankOn's fork of
Bazaar:

    https://github.com/BlankOn/bazaar  (branch main)

which is GNOME World's Bazaar (https://gitlab.gnome.org/World/bazaar) plus
the downstream "APT system updates" patch and the "Warsi rebrand", kept in
the fork itself (see
ai-patches/PLAN.md there). Nothing is patched here: debian/patches is empty
on purpose, because the fork is where downstream code changes live.
The snapshot packaged by the current debian/changelog entry is commit
f6652e48 (the rebrand itself is 22976690, reworded in 6639f360; renderer
choice 558323cd and e2eed3a7; search keywords 75a05c98; .deb installer
45f2b9bb; new icon 2bb49120; wordmark a62e05ec; GNOME Extensions tab f0c2ee74, its
bus name fix 88546928).

The version 0.10.1~gitYYYYMMDDHHMM.<commit> means "a snapshot before upstream
0.10.1 is released": upstream's version.sh already says 0.10.1, the last tag
is v0.10.0, and "~" sorts the snapshot before a real 0.10.1.

Layout of this repository
-------------------------

This repository holds debian/ only. The upstream source comes from the
--source repository given to irgsh-cli: irgsh-cli clones it at
--source-branch, packs it as warsi_<version>.orig.tar.xz (the upstream part
of debian/changelog's version), then runs "dpkg-source --build" here. The
builder unpacks the .dsc and builds the binaries from that.

So:

  * The packaged code is whatever --source-branch points to when you
    submit. Bump debian/changelog to match the commit you package (version
    0.10.1~gitYYYYMMDDHHMM.<short-commit>-1).
  * Code changes go to the fork (github.com/BlankOn/bazaar), not here.
    debian/patches is not used.
  * dpkg-source prints "ignoring deletion of file ..." for every upstream
    file, because they are in the orig tarball but not in this directory.
    That is expected and harmless.
  * Do not add files outside debian/ (a top-level .gitignore, for example):
    with 3.0 (quilt) a file that differs from the orig tarball aborts the
    source build ("aborting due to unexpected upstream changes"). The
    top-level README.md, about this packaging, is the exception: it is
    listed in debian/source/options (extend-diff-ignore), so dpkg-source
    ignores it and the package keeps upstream's README.md. Add any other
    such file to that pattern. Ignore build products with .git/info/exclude instead:

        printf '/obj-*/\n/debian/.debhelper/\n/debian/debhelper-build-stamp\n/debian/files\n/debian/*.substvars\n/debian/*.debhelper.log\n/debian/warsi/\n/debian/libbge0/\n/debian/libbge-dev/\n/debian/bge-demo/\n/debian/tmp/\n/.pc/\n' >> .git/info/exclude

Packaging a new upstream snapshot
---------------------------------

    C=<commit>
    dch -v 0.10.1~git$(git -C ../bazaar log -1 --format=%cd --date=format:%Y%m%d%H%M $C).$(git -C ../bazaar rev-parse --short=8 $C)-1 "New upstream snapshot."

The commit time (YYYYMMDDHHMM, not only the date) is part of the version
on purpose: two snapshots made on the same day would otherwise be ordered
by their commit hashes, which is random (…20261115.67a3245a sorts before
…20261115.c1115789). Since 0.10.1~git202611151833 the time is always
included; never go back to a date-only version, it would sort lower.
Check before uploading:

    dpkg --compare-versions <new> gt <previous> && echo ok

then submit with --source-branch pointing at that commit's branch.

Building and submitting
-----------------------

Submit to irgsh (both repositories over https; the package repository is
this one, pushed somewhere public):

    irgsh-cli package \
        --source https://github.com/BlankOn/bazaar \
        --source-branch main \
        --package <https URL of this repository> \
        --package-branch master \
        --skip-local-build \
        --experimental

--skip-local-build is needed when the build dependencies are installed on
the submitting machine: irgsh-cli then tries a local binary build of this
directory, which has no source in it, so that build cannot work. (Without
the build dependencies installed, irgsh-cli skips that build by itself.)
Drop --experimental to inject into the official dev repository.
irgsh-cli checks that Uploaders in debian/control and the last
debian/changelog entry match the identity of the signing key.

Local test build, the way the builder does it (needs the Build-Depends;
"sudo mk-build-deps -ir debian/control" installs them):

    V=0.10.1~git202611170130.ebeddb2b
    git -C ../bazaar archive --prefix=warsi-$V/ <commit> | xz > ../warsi_$V.orig.tar.xz
    dpkg-source --build .
    cd .. && dpkg-source -x warsi_$V-1.dsc && cd warsi-$V
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b

Binary packages
---------------

The split follows Debian's own bazaar packaging (0.9.4-2). The app package
is named warsi and installs no file of Debian's bazaar package (different
program names and app ID), so the two can be installed together. The
libbge packages keep their names and replace Debian's:

  warsi         the app, its daemon, download worker and data files
  libbge0       libbge.so.0, the "Bazaar GTK Extensions" library
  libbge-dev    libbge headers, .so link and pkg-config file
  bge-demo      demo program for libbge

packagekit is a Recommends of warsi: without it the System Updates card
stays hidden and Warsi behaves like upstream Bazaar.
